  "response": "The dispute begins from a datum that both parties accept. The original effect was not detected in the replication study; that is, the replication study produced no evidence of the effect reported in the original report. What is contested is not this outcome but its diagnosis, and psychologists have assembled a mixture of empirical, procedural and statistical resources for settling the question.\n\nLogically, at least one of two things must hold of such a case, and perhaps both. The effect reported in the original study was inflated by selective reporting of analyses, or the replication procedure departed from the original procedure in a theoretically relevant respect. Whereas the first diagnosis locates the fault in the original record and its reporting practices, the second locates it in the conditions under which the effect was sought: the hidden moderator hypothesis attributes replication failure to unmeasured contextual differences between the original and replication settings. In other words, to say that the replication departed from the original in a theoretically relevant respect is to put the hidden moderator's claim in procedural terms. Adjudication therefore turns on evidence about how much setting actually matters, and here the hidden-moderator account cannot be squared with what the largest multi-site projects have measured. Many Labs 2 found that effect sizes varied little across samples and research settings. The project concluded that cultural variation across sites explained most replication failures.\n\nA second line of adjudication is procedural rather than empirical. Daniel Kahneman proposed a \"replication etiquette\" obliging replicators to consult the authors of the original study, and that proposal resulted in the editorial machinery of Registered Replication Reports. Registered Replication Reports require the replication protocol to be reviewed by the original authors before data collection begins. Under this format, original authors are prohibited from any involvement in the replication process.\n\nStatistical criteria supply a third resource, since the two diagnoses differ in what they predict about the magnitude of the effect. The small telescopes approach tests whether the true effect is too small to have been detectable by the original study's sample size; put differently, equivalence testing permits researchers to conclude that an effect is smaller than a prespecified bound. A replication that licenses such a conclusion shifts the burden onto the proponent of a moderator to name it and measure it.\n\nThe practical force of these conventions is clearest in disputed cases. Because Simone Schnall objected to a failed replication of her cleanliness priming study on the grounds that the replication's measures suffered from ceiling effects, the journal's editorial review board was brought to conclude that the ceiling-effect objection did not account for that failed cleanliness priming replication. The general lesson is that the choice between a nonexistent effect and an unfaithful reproduction stops being a matter of competing assertion only when protocols are fixed and vetted in advance, when putative moderators are measured rather than merely invoked, and when the smallest effect the original design could have detected is stated explicitly.",
